Input: {"question": "What year was the Married Women's Property Act passed?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d00", "start": 0, "end": 86, "text": "Married Women's Property Act 1870 The Married Women's Property Act 1870 (33 & 34 Vict."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d00", "start": 87, "end": 239, "text": "c.93) was an Act of Parliament of the United Kingdom that allowed married women to be the legal owners of the money they earned and to inherit property."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d00", "start": 240, "end": 421, "text": "Before 1870, any money made by a woman either through a wage, from investment, by gift, or through inheritance automatically became the property of her husband once she was married."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d00", "start": 422, "end": 552, "text": "Thus, the identity of the wife became legally absorbed into that of her husband, effectively making them one person under the law."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d00", "start": 553, "end": 580, "text": "Once a woman became married"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d01", "start": 0, "end": 622, "text": "Toggle the table of contents Married Women 's Property Act 1870 2 languages বাংলা Deutsch Edit links Article Talk English Read Edit View history Tools Tools move to sidebar hide Actions Read Edit View history General What links here Related changes Upload file Permanent link Page information Cite this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Wikidata item Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wikipedia , the free encyclopedia United Kingdom legislation Married Women 's Property Act 1870 [ 1 ] Act of Parliament Parliament of the United Kingdom Long title"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d02", "start": 0, "end": 86, "text": "Married Women's Property Act 1882 The Married Women's Property Act 1882 (45 & 46 Vict."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d02", "start": 87, "end": 328, "text": "c.75) was an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom that significantly altered English law regarding the property rights of married women, which besides other matters allowed married women to own and control property in their own right."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d02", "start": 329, "end": 412, "text": "The Act applied in England (and Wales) and Ireland, but did not extend to Scotland."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d02", "start": 413, "end": 511, "text": "The Married Women’s Property Act was a model for similar legislation in other British territories."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d02", "start": 512, "end": 615, "text": "For example, Victoria passed legislation in 1884, New South Wales in 1889, and the remaining Australian"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d03", "start": 0, "end": 41, "text": "same property rights as unmarried women ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d03", "start": 42, "end": 142, "text": "[ 9 ] A long and energetic campaign by women 's groups and some men led to the passing of this Act ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d03", "start": 143, "end": 463, "text": "The Married Women 's Property Act 1870 provided that wages and property which a wife earned through her own work or inherited would be regarded as her separate property and by the Married Women 's Property Act 1882 , this principle was extended to all property , regardless of its source or the time of its acquisition ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d03", "start": 464, "end": 515, "text": "[ 10 ] The Act also protected a woman not only from"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d04", "start": 0, "end": 7, "text": "status."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d04", "start": 8, "end": 166, "text": "In 1845, New York granted a married woman who secured \"a patent for her own invention\" the right to hold it and retain all earnings from it \"as if unmarried\"."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d04", "start": 167, "end": 360, "text": "The Married Women's Property Act was enacted on April 7, 1848, as part of a more general movement, underway since the 1820s, away from common law traditions in favor of the codification of law."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d04", "start": 361, "end": 492, "text": "Ernestine Rose had been campaigning for such a statute since 1836, later joined by Paulina Wright Davis and Elizabeth Cady Stanton."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d04", "start": 493, "end": 590, "text": "It significantly altered the law regarding the property rights granted to married women, allowing"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e:d05", "start": 0, "end": 632, "text": "65535 f 0000000081 65535 f 0000000082 65535 f 0000000083 65535 f 0000000084 65535 f 0000000085 65535 f 0000000086 65535 f 0000000087 65535 f 0000000088 65535 f 0000000089 65535 f 0000000090 65535 f 0000000091 65535 f 0000000092 65535 f 0000000093 65535 f 0000000094 65535 f 0000000095 65535 f 0000000096 65535 f 0000000097 65535 f 0000000098 65535 f 0000000099 65535 f 0000000100 65535 f 0000000101 65535 f 0000000102 65535 f 0000000103 65535 f 0000000104 65535 f 0000000105 65535 f 0000000106 65535 f 0000000107 65535 f 0000000108 65535 f 0000000109 65535 f 0000000110 65535 f 0000000111 65535 f 0000000112 65535 f 0000000113 65535"}, "D7S1": 